+++ b/archive/yin-vm/1790060000000-reviewer-debruijn-b1.qwen3.8-max.findings.md
@@ -0,0 +1 @@
+"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-22 08:20:00 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-22 15:20:00 Indochina Time\nCoding-Agent: cmd\nSession-ID: pending\n\n## Verdict: NOT READY\n\nTwo P1s, both defeating guarantees this exact phase exists to provide (the sole admitter of images, and host-identical hashed bytes). Both fixes are small and local; a re-review after them should be fast.\n\n---\n\n## P1 findings\n\n**P1-1. `:str` operands bypass the UTF-16 surrogate guard — unrefused, host-divergent hashed bytes.**\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:482` (`:str (framed :string (or x \"\"))` in `encode-operand`).\nDesign violated (yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md:168-171): \"Unpaired UTF-16 surrogates are refused with `:unsupported-value`, as they are by the projection's UTF-16 guard. Values outside the declared executable domain are refused with a qualified `unsupported-value` diagnostic, not silently canonicalized.\"\nConcrete scenario: `[[:gensym \"\\uD800\"] [:return]]` passes `image-defect` (the `:str` kind check is only `string?`, line 607), then `image-hash` hashes it without refusal. `jing/sha256` UTF-8-encodes the lone surrogate three different ways — JVM `.getBytes \"UTF-8\"` emits `0x3F` (and the `utf8-byte-length` prefix says 3, so even the JVM's own framing is internally wrong), CLJS `stringToUtf8ByteArray` emits a 3-byte CESU-like encoding, Dart `utf8.encode` throws. Same image → different H (or a crash) per host: a silent identity fork, exactly what S2 forbids. The `:const` path refuses correctly (`scalar-class` line 355 → nil → qualified throw lines 442-443), which proves the asymmetry is an oversight, not a decision.\nSmallest fix: at line 482, route non-nil `:str` through the guarded encoder — `:str (if (nil? x) (framed :string \"\") (encode-scalar x))` — or add an explicit `well-formed-utf16?` ch
eck throwing the same `:unsupported-value` ex-info.\n\n**P1-2. The scope validator accepts a body declared by two closures with conflicting arities — a malformed image gets through the sole admitter.**\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:724-730` (`all-body-chains` skips an entry when `(contains? owner entry)`, discarding the second declared chain) and `:703` (`walk-body` likewise drops re-reach under a different chain, e.g. via a cross-body `:jump`).\nDesign violated (yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md:250-252): \"B1's image validator repeats the check by walking each body's declared arity and enclosing-body chain\"; (254-255) \"only the B1 image validator admits executable images.\"\nConcrete scenario: `[[:closure 2 3] [:closure 1 3] [:return] [:load-bound 0 1] [:return]]` → `image-defect` returns nil (I traced it: the BFS records chain `'(2)` at pc 3 first; the second declaration `'(1)` is silently dropped; position 1 < 2 validates). But the arity-1 closure at pc 1 declares the same body; called through it, §4's `(vec (take arity ...))` frame has 1 slot and `[:load-bound 0 1]` reads out of range at runtime. Note also the walk-order dependence: swapping the two `:closure` instructions makes the *same* `:load-bound` be rejected — validation outcome depends on which declaration is traversed first. B6 receives foreign images over the network, so this is the live threat model, not a lowerer concern (B2 emits fresh bodies).\nSmallest fix: in `all-body-chains`, when the dequeued `entry` is already in `owner` with a chain *different* from the recorded one, return a defect (e.g. `{:rule :scope-conflict :pc entry}`) instead of skipping. The fuller fix keeps a set of chains per pc and validates each `:load-bound` under every chain that reaches it (covering the cross-body-`jump` variant).\n\n## P2 findings\n\n**P2-1. JVM `host-double?` silently folds BigDecimal/Float to the nearest double.**\n`debruijn_code.cljc:328-336` (\"every remaining number\") plus `:234` (`(Double/doubleToLongBits (double v))`).\nDesign violated (yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md:166-167, 170-171): \"Numeric bits ... are used exactly as supplied\" and \"Values outside the declared executable domain are refused ..., not silently canonicalized.\" The 14-class domain (doc:163-164) has no BigDecimal/Float class, and `vm/plain-data?` (`vm.cljc:726`) admits any `(number? x)` into `:data` slots, so these values pass `image-defect`.\nScenario: `(encode-scalar 0.1000000000000000000001M)` and `(encode-scalar 0.1M)` produce identical bytes — two distinct JVM values, one hash. Same for `(float 1.5)` vs `1.5`.\nSmallest fix: on the JVM make `host-double?` require `(instance? Double v)` (CLJS/CLJD keep the current complement rule, which is correct there); BigDecimal/Float then classify nil → qualified `:unsupported-value`. (The projection's `numeric-class` folds via `float?` too, but that is the dormant table's own domain decision; this dimension's contract explicitly disclaims silent canonicalization.)\n\n**P2-2. No test exercises any `:unsupported-value` refusal path — on any host.**\n`test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc` (whole file): the qualified throw at `debruijn_code.cljc:442-443`, the map/set collision throws (`:432-441`), and the surrogate refusal are never triggered. The lone-surrogate fixture is constructible on all three lanes (JVM `\"\\uD800\"`, CLJS `(js/String.fromCharCode 0xD800)`, CLJD equivalent), so S2's surrogate rule (doc:168-169) is testable cross-host and untested. P1-1 survived precisely because no refusal test pins the behavior at the second entry point. (The ratio-refusal side on CLJS/CLJD is legitimately untestable — the value can't be constructed there — and the host-boundary test at `:272-291` checking the declared support set is a reasonable substitute for that one.)\nSmallest fix: one cross-host deftest asserting the ex-info `{:rule :unsupported-value}` for `(dc/encode-scalar <lone-surrogate string>)` and for `dc/image-hash` of `[[:const <lone-surrogate>] [:return]]`, plus (after the P1-1 fix) `[[:gensym <lone-surrogate>] [:return]]`.\n\n## P3 findings\n\n**P3-1. `saturated-operands` is hand-copied and drops `[:ffi-call 2]`.**\n`debruijn_code.cljc:630` vs the source `code.cljc:242` (`#{[:gensym 1] [:stream-make 1] [:call 2] [:ffi-call 2]}`). No live gap: this dimension's stricter `:uint nonneg-int?` kind check (`:610`) rejects a nil ffi-argc as `:operand-kind` instead. But the opcode table is derived-as-data while this set is copied, so a future shape change to `:ffi-call` (or relaxing the `:uint` check) turns the omission into a gap. Fix: add `[:ffi-call 2]`, or derive the set from the source table's saturated operands for carried mnemonics.\n\n**P3-2. Ratio components outside long range throw unqualified.**\n`debruijn_code.cljc:423-424`: JVM Ratio numerator/denominator are BigIntegers; for e.g. `(/ 10000000000000000000N 3)`, `(long …)` inside `int64-le-hex` throws `IllegalArgumentException` (\"Value out of range for long\") — a refusal, but not the \"qualified `unsupported-value` diagnostic\" the design (doc:170-171) requires. Fix: range-check the components and throw the qualified ex-info, or encode them through the `:bigint` text rule.\n\n**P3-3. Cross-host validator divergence on unsafe-integer `:uint` operands (CLJS).**\n`debruijn_code.cljc:595-597` + `:483`: on CLJS `(integer? 1e30)` is true, so `[:call 1e30 true]` passes `image-defect` and `int64-le-hex`'s CLJS floor/mod decomposition (`:214-220`) produces silently inexact bytes beyond 2^53; on JVM the same image is rejected (`integer?` false for a Double). One image, valid on one host, invalid on another — against the \"only admitter\" consistency the sharing path needs. Fix: bound `:uint` (and `:pc`) operands to the safe-integer range in `nonneg-int?`.\n\n**P3-4. Hashed descriptor contains prose.**\n`debruijn_code.cljc:128` (the `:image-hash` formula string) and `:138` (the lift-morphism sentence) flow through `encode-scalar descriptor` → `descriptor-hash` → every H. A copy-edit of either sentence forks the identity of every image, even though `lowering-contract-version` already guards shape changes. The design (doc:153-155) requires the descriptor to *declare* these things, not to hash their prose. Fix: keep the hashed values as data (keywords/versions), move the sentences into docstrings.\n\n**P3-5. `image-scalar-classes` scans only `:const`, missing the `:data` operands of `:store-get`/`:store-put`.**\n`debruijn_code.cljc:537`. This follows the design's own sentence (doc:198, \"derived from tags in its hashed `:const` operands\"), so it is a design-level under-approximation the code faithfully inherits — but the consequence is real: a JVM-produced `[[:store-put \"k\" 1/2] [:return]]` has `:ratio` bytes in its hash, yet `validate-host-support` on CLJS/CLJD reports nothing missing, so S2's receiver refusal (doc:200-202) cannot fire from this scan. Fix (cheap, strictly more conservative): scan every `:data` operand, or record the `:const`-only limitation as an explicit design decision for B6.\n\n## Checked and clean\n\n- **Opcode table derivation (genuine, not hand-copied).** `carried-mnemonics = (disj code/mnemonics :var :closure)` (`:61-65`); each carried entry is `renamespace-slots` over `(get code/vector-operand-table m)` (`:78-81`) — kinds and order read straight off the source table, attributes moved to `:yin.debruijn.code/*`. All 21 source mnemonics accounted for: 19 carried, `:closure` redefined, `:var` split. New shapes match the design (doc:148-152) exactly: `:load-bound [depth :uint, position :uint]`, `:load-free [name :sym]`, `:closure [arity :uint, body :pc]` (`:82-84`); the `:syms` kind correctly vanishes with the old params slot. Test `:232-249` pins the derivation against the *live* source table, so drift in `yin.vm.code` fails the suite. I hand-recomputed the sorted mnemonic tag indices (const=03, closure=02, load-bound=09, load-free=0a, return=0e over the 22-mnemonic sorted vocabulary) and the full byte strings of all four golden fixtures (tags, 8-hex length prefixes, LE int64s, ident framing) — they match the pinned literals.\n- **No private-var reach.** Requires are `clojure.set`, `dao.jing`, `yin.vm`, `yin.vm.code` only (`:41-45`); every qualified call is public (`jing/sha256`, `vm/plain-data?`, `code/mnemonics`, `code/vector-operand-table`, `code/terminators`); all `yin.vm.debruijn/` mentions are prose. The copied helpers (`to-hex :154`, `code-unit-at :168`, `utf8-byte-length :175`, `framed :200`, `int64-le-hex :208`, `double-le-hex :226`, `well-formed-utf16? :247`) are faithful to `yin.vm.debruijn:775-872, 228-253`, differing only where intended (own `class-tag`).\n- **Scalar encoder core rules.** No NFC anywhere; `ident-content` frames ns/name exactly with `:nil` for an absent namespace (`:390-396`, so `sym` ≠ `ns/sym`); no integral-double folding (JVM `instance?` longs; CLJS safe-integer per doc:179-181; CLJD `int?` matching `yin.vm.debruijn/numeric-class`'s Dart rule); map/set sorted by encoded bytes with collision guards; cross-host double bytes verified consistent (LE bits, one canonical quiet NaN `000000000000f87f`, CLJD BE-ByteData-read-in-reverse matches the original); CLJS negative-long decomposition through floor/mod traced correct (`-1` → `ff`×8).\n- **Host classification, corrected parts.** `host-char?`, `host-ratio?`, `host-bigint?` are `false` on CLJD and CLJS with `:cljd` listed FIRST (`:284, :296, :313-315`) — matching both the corrected design wording (doc:184-196: char JVM-only because CLJD `char?` can't be trusted; ratio/bigint JVM-only, refused elsewhere) and handoff.md:212-215's trap guidance. `host-long?`/`host-double?`/`code-unit-at`/`double-le-hex` put `:clj` first but carry explicit `:cljd` branches — the same order as production precedent (`yin.vm.debruijn:220-232, 866-872`), which the passing Dart lane proves the emitter honors. I audited every `#?()` in both files: none relies on a `:default` fallthrough where a `:clj` branch is present; `int64-le-hex`'s `:cljs`/`:default` has no `:clj` branch and its default is the portable bit-op code; `encode-scalar`'s `:ratio` branch guards `numerator`/`denominator` behind explicit `:cljs`/`:cljd` branches so Dart never resolves them; the ns-form import is `:cljd`-only. The test file's three JVM-only deftests use \"unconditional deftest with conditional body\" — the exact spelling orchestrator-log.md:1636-1638 prescribes for CLJD — so the `1/2`, `\\a`, and `…N` literals never reach the Dart emitter (precedent: `test/dao/gui/event/fixture_test.cljc:56,74,87`).\n- **CLJS integral-double rule not conflated with encode.** The encode side implements \"long iff safe integer, else double\"; the receive-side refusal is explicitly documented as a later decode path (docstring `:34-40`) — correct scoping, since B1 has no wire decoder (B6's obligation; see P3-5 for the scan gap it inherits).\n- **`image-hash` = H, alone.** `sha256(descriptor-hash ‖ encode-image v)` (`:511-524`), descriptor-hash folding in `lowering-contract-version` (`:106-110, :122`) — matches D9/D14. Exactly two sha256 call sites; no projection fingerprint, no second identity. Nothing diagnostic in the hashed bytes: no side table exists in this file; hashed content is descriptor data plus positional tuples only. `jing/sha256` hashes UTF-8 bytes of the string on all three hosts (`dao/jing.cljc:271-288`), so the mixed hex+raw preimage is host-identical for well-formed UTF-16 content (the one bypass is P1-1).\n- **Validator structural rules.** Mirror `yin.vm.code`'s §7.5 rules 1-7 with the argc rule folded into a stricter `:uint` kind check; rule ordering is safe (mnemonic precedes arity, so a nil table lookup can't reach it); root-body `:load-bound` rejected (depth 0 ≥ count of `'()`); dangling jump/closure-body caught by `target-bounds`; `:load-bound` at an unreached pc rejected via nil chain (`:748-749`); nested-closure chains innermost-first, consistent with §4's frame order conversion.\n- **Tests vs the B1 box.** Composed (line 95, U+00E9) vs decomposed (line 96, U+0065 U+0301) é verified in the raw file bytes — a real no-NFC proof; `1`/`1.0` tested on JVM+Dart and excluded on CLJS exactly as the corrected design requires (doc:193-195); char and ratio distinct-hash tests are JVM-only, matching both the design and the code's actual predicates (neither stricter nor looser); the golden fixtures pin implementation-independent byte and hash literals covering `:const`, `:closure`/`:load-bound`, and `:load-free`/`:sym` — any tag-order, framing, mnemonic-order, or descriptor perturbation shifts the pinned strings and fails on every lane, so the load-bearing claim holds by construction, not by assertion."
