@@ -0,0 +1,28 @@
+No session ID is exposed to me programmatically, so I'll note that in the report. Work is complete — writing the final report now.
+
+## Report
+
+Completed-GMT: 2026-09-22 07:35:51 GMT
+Completed-Local: 2026-09-22 14:35:51 +07 (Indochina Time)
+Coding-Agent: claude
+Session-ID: not exposed to this agent (no env var or tool surfaces it in this harness)
+
+**VM state shape and closure representation.** `DebruijnVM` (`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_vm.cljc`) carries exactly the design's 8 fields (`:segment :pc :frames :free-env :stack :continuation :store :status`) plus two composition-level fields not part of the transition state: `:primitives` and `:modules`, needed to give `:load-free` the same `env -> store -> primitives -> module registry` order `engine/resolve-var` already implements — mirrored on `SemanticVM`, which likewise carries fields beyond its four CESK-state accessors. A closure value is `{:type :closure, :arity n, :body-pc pc, :frames captured-frames}`; it carries no `:macro?` field, confirmed by inspection of section 2's instruction shape — this is a positional dimension and never had one.
+
+**Frame direction.** Frame 0 for `:load-bound` is the innermost frame, read from the end of `:frames`. Concrete example from `nested-closure-capture-test`: after the outer closure `(fn [x] ...)` is called with `3` and the inner closure `(fn [y] ...)` is called with `4`, `:frames` is `[[3] [4]]`. Inside the inner body, `[:load-bound 1 0]` reaches `x` (depth 1 → index `2-1-1=0` → frame `[3]`) and `[:load-bound 0 0]` reaches `y` (depth 0 → index `1` → frame `[4]`).
+
+**Reuse vs. fresh.** Reused directly: `yin.vm.engine/resolve-var` for `:load-free` (signature `[env store primitives registry name]` fit `:free-env`/`:store`/`:primitives`/`:modules` without adaptation, so no equivalent was written). Written fresh: `bind-positional` (the positional `bind-params` equivalent, `(vec (take arity (concat args (repeat nil))))`), and `frame-value` (innermost-at-end addressing) — both new, positional-only concepts with no named-VM analogue to call. `:call`'s primitive-function branch (`(apply f args)`) is a minimal fresh reimplementation of the pure half of `apply-call`'s `fn?` case (`yin.vm.semantic.cljc`), without its effect-handling branch, since FFI/effects are B4's.
+
+**Completion criteria.**
+- Pure-program parity, fresh VM instances, B0's normalizer: `test/yin/vm/debruijn_vm_test.cljc`, every `deftest` compares `(named-value ast)` against `(debruijn-value segment)` through `yin.vm.debruijn-vm-contract-test/normalize`, each call building a brand-new `tu/create-vm`/`dvm/create-vm`.
+- Corpus, hand-written instruction vectors: `literal-test` (const); `load-bound-identity-test` (bound load, arity 1); `load-free-primitive-test` (free load via primitives, plus `:call` applying a resolved host fn) and `load-free-store-fallback-test` (free load falling through to store — not compared against the named VM, since it exercises the internal resolution-order decision rather than a distinct AST shape; asserted directly); `nested-closure-capture-test` (nested closures, depth-1 bound load); `zero-arity-closure-test` (0-arity, multiple-arities coverage); `under-arity-call-nil-fills-test` / `over-arity-call-drops-extras-test` (nil-fill and extras-dropped, arity 2, built on a shared `conj-pair-ast`/`conj-pair-segment` fixture); `branch-true-arm-test` / `branch-false-arm-test` (both `if` arms); `store-get-put-sequenced-test` (put then get, sequenced through the named AST's application-operand-order rule since the Universal AST has no explicit sequencing node). `fresh-instance-per-run-test` reconfirms self-parity across two fresh instances. `not-yet-implemented-opcode-test` and `environment-not-implemented-test` cover the two "must fail loudly / must not implement" requirements (out-of-scope opcode, `IVMState/environment`).
+- Benchmark report: skipped per the design ("not an acceptance condition" for B3) and per the collab brief's explicit instruction not to spend time on it.
+
+**What ran, exact counts.** `clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.debruijn-vm-test`: 14 tests, 23 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors. `clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.debruijn-vm-contract-test` (B0, unmodified, re-run to confirm nothing regressed): 10 tests, 84 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors.
+
+**What I could not run.** `cljstyle check` on the two new files: the Bash call requiring approval was denied by the user, so per the collab brief's instruction I did not retry and did not use `--dangerously-skip-permissions`. `clj-kondo` is not on `PATH` in this environment, so it was never attempted. Node/CLJS and ClojureDart lanes were not run — the collab brief's Environment section only names the focused JVM run, and this whole phase is `.cljc` with no host-conditional code, so I did not seek those lanes out separately.
+
+**Deviations.**
+- `:store-put`'s AST-side `:val` is a literal value, not an AST node to evaluate (confirmed by reading `yin.vm/ast_walker.cljc:419` and the `ast->datoms` encoding) — my first draft of `store-get-put-sequenced-test` wrongly wrapped it in `(lit 123)`; fixed to a bare `123` once the mismatch showed up as a test failure.
+- `:call` handles a resolved primitive host function (`fn? f` branch, `(apply f args)`) in addition to `:closure` values. This is not explicitly listed among the raw instruction shapes in the collab brief, but without it `:load-free` (explicitly in scope, item 5) would resolve a callable value with no way to invoke it. This mirrors the pure half of the named engine's own `apply-call` `fn?` branch; I did not add any effect handling (module/effect? dispatch), which stays out of scope for B4.
+- No other deviations: no existing file was edited; `vm.cljc`, `engine.cljc`, `semantic.cljc`, `code.cljc`, and the merged projection namespace are all untouched (confirmed by `git status` showing only the two new files plus the pre-existing untracked collab prompt and `node_modules`).
